      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"I am.\" (Soy.) You learned ser three chapters ago as the verb for saying what someone is."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And then you had nothing to put after it. Today you do."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "profesor — a teacher."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin profitērī meant to declare something publicly. A profesor is someone who has declared what they know — which is also why in English you profess a faith, and why the work you declare yourself trained in is a profession."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "profesor from profitērī becomes professor, profess, profession."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say it: profesor. And for a woman, profesora."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the missing \"a\"",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "After ser, a job takes no un or una."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You learned un and una early, and you have been right to use them. Here is the one place Spanish leaves them out."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Spanish",
                "English"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "Soy profesor. means I am a teacher.",
                "Es profesora. means She is a teacher."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English needs the a. Spanish does not — because it is not counting one teacher, it is naming what kind of person you are."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Soy un profesor is not wrong so much as different: it makes the teacher into one item out of several, the way English does with I'm a teacher, not the teacher. Say the bare one and you will sound like everyone else."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
